== The product decision you are implementing ==
Overload is controlled by backpressure alone. Nothing rejects work because a queue is full.

- Core controls with its byte HWM. Framework controls with the number of jobs queued.
- One Framework host owns ONE job-permit limit, `MaxQueuedApplicationJobs`. However many
  MeshNodes, ClientServer channels, sockets or connections the host has, they share that one
  limit, and only one place in a host counts permits.
- At 80% of that limit the host applies `PAUSED` to every supported socket; at or below 60%
  it applies `RUNNING`. Supported sockets are the RouteMesh ROUTER-ROUTER socket and the
  ClientServer DEALER-ROUTER socket.
- If usage still reaches 100%, the host stops receiving. What it does not receive stays in
  the local Core queue, Core hits its byte limit, and the sender is held there.
- NOTHING IS DROPPED OR REJECTED. Everything received goes to a handler.
- Sending is a single call to the Core socket's send or request. The Framework builds no
  waiting queue of its own, does not stage backpressure, and does not retry. Waiting belongs
  to Core and the binding. If the wait runs out of time the call ends with `DeadlineExceeded`
  — send, publish, one-way and request alike.

== What that means for the code: three separate deletions ==

**(1) The completion-slot cap.** Sending a request reserved a completion slot and rejected
when the reservation failed. The cap is gone. So is the reservation itself: it existed only
to protect against a rejection the cap created, and with no cap there is nothing to protect
against. What stays is the REGISTRATION that §10 of the submit spec requires for a different
reason — the pending entry, reply route and dispatcher slot are registered before the
transport submit so a reply that arrives immediately is never processed before its
registration. That is an ordering rule, not a capacity rule. A runtime may still pre-allocate
the queue node to keep the terminal path allocation-free; that is a cost choice it owns.

**(2) Every queue limit other than the host job permit, and every "queue is full, so fail"
path that went with it.**

**There is exactly one limit left in the Framework: `MaxQueuedApplicationJobs`, the number of
jobs a host has waiting for a handler.** Bytes are Core's business — Core's byte HWM bounds
what a queue holds, and when this host stops reading, those bytes hold the sender back. The
Framework counts items, at one place per host, and nowhere else.

So these all go, along with the failures they produced:

| Limit | Where |
|---|---|
| completion slot 4,096 | operation registry and completion dispatcher |
| application lane 1,024 items / 64 MiB | per-execution-object FIFO |
| lifecycle lane 128 items / 4 MiB | per-execution-object FIFO |
| mailbox count and bytes | owner mailbox |
| worker scheduler queue bound | worker pool |
| any slot for holding a reply | wherever it is kept |

Delete the fields, the constructor parameters, the options that carry them, and every branch
that failed when they were reached. Leave no branch that can never be taken.

**These are limits too, but they stay — they are not queue capacity:**

| | Why it stays |
|---|---|
| Per-node Actor/Spot limit, per-stable-type limit | "How many Spots may live on this node", not a queue. Waiting does not create one — it ends with `Unavailable` |
| `MaxMessageSize` | The size of one message, not the length of a queue |
| Pending activation 128 | Activations in progress |
| Owner time budget 10 ms, lifecycle 8 consecutive turns | Fairness, not capacity — they stop one owner from holding the turn |

Separating the application and lifecycle lanes also stays. That separation orders and
prioritises work; it never bounded anything.

**Do not remove a public API, builder option, or configuration surface that is not one of the
deleted limits.** If a change seems to need one, stop and report it under BLOCKERS.

**(3) The `CapacityExceeded` error kind itself.** With no cap anywhere, nothing can produce
it. It is removed from the `ErrorKind` enum and the values after it shift down:

```
0 NotFound        1 AlreadyExists   2 TypeMismatch    3 NotConfigured
4 Rejected        5 Unavailable     6 DeadlineExceeded
7 ShuttingDown    8 ProtocolError   9 InvalidOperation
10 DataLost       11 InternalFailure
```

Twelve kinds, 0..11. Every existing producer moves:

| Old producer | New result |
|---|---|
| A queue, table or slot was full | Not an error. Wait; `DeadlineExceeded` if the time runs out |
| No node can host the Spot (placement) | `Unavailable` |
| HTTP client response body exceeded its configured size | `Rejected` |

== Explicitly OUT OF SCOPE — do not touch, and report it if you are tempted ==
